    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 282321\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n421 PM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Last day of dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert \n  toDAY before the start of a cooling trend. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region through \n  Saturday, with gusty winds and heavy rain being the greatest \n  threat.\n\n* Gusty winds Saturday afternoon with sporadic gusts 35-40+ mph. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Next Thursday\nA pattern shift is on the way for the next week for the Desert \nSouthwest bringing an end to the extreme heat and record breaking \ntemperatures for late summer. Strong 500 mb ridging which has been \nthe dominant weather influence over the past week finally shifts \neastward today as a series of troughs pushes into the west coast \ninto next week bringing relatively cooler and drier conditions.\n\nFor today, a 594dm upper ridge is centered over the Four Corners \nregion while an upper trough slowly pushes into the west coast. \nSatellite imagery clearly depicts the jet streak and associated \nmoisture return and clouds ahead of the trough stretching from the \nPacific to Central California and into Central and Northern Nevada. \nThe presence of clouds today has already kept temperatures about 5-8 \ndegrees cooler than they were yesterday at this time, but even then \ntemperatures are still above normal for this time of year. In fact, \nyesterday, Las Vegas broke their daily record high, reaching 111F \nand also broke a record warm low temperature at 92F! Yesterday was \nofficially the latest date at which Las Vegas has ever observed a \nlow temperature at or above 90 degrees.  Despite a slow downward \ntrend in temperatures into the weekend, they will still remain \nseveral degrees above normal for late summer. An Extreme Heat \nWarning remains in effect through this evening for the Mojave \nDesert. While afternoon high temperatures have been this hot (or \nhotter) earlier in the summer without heat headlines, it is \nimportant to note that there is historically a resurgence of heat-\nrelated impacts at the end of the season. See the CLIMATE section at \nthe bottom for daily records. \n\nA monsoonal pattern still remains in place for the Southwest ahead \nof the approaching trough. Moisture advection northward has been \nmuch slower than what guidance has suggested the past few days, but \nit appears somewhat deeper moisture has finally surged northward. \nDew points today are running about 10 degrees higher than they were \nyesterday with widespread 50s observed. High clouds have kept most \nconvection at bay early this afternoon with the exception of \nnorthern Mohave County where better instability and a weak mid/upper \nwave has sparked off showers and thunderstorms. While storm motion \nhas been at or above 20 kts, storm motion parallel to the mean flow \nis causing training, which may increase the Flash Flood threat for \nthe rest of the day. Already seeing afternoon cu develop across \nSouthern Nevada in areas receiving more sunshine and surface \nheating. Instability is still only marginal across Nevada and SE \nCalifornia, which may keep convective coverage lower than what CAMs \nguidance suggests for later this afternoon. Outside of Mohave \nCounty, best chances for storms look to be in the higher terrain. \nAny storm has the capability of producing heavy rain, lightning, \ngusty winds, and flash flooding. As the trough slowly pushes east \ntomorrow, drier air advects in from the northwest; however enough \nmoisture should still be around in portions of Northern Mohave \nCounty and Lincoln counties to support additional convective \ndevelopment. Similar set up tomorrow but with better upper level \ndynamics, supporting more organized clusters of storms and the \npossibility for training and flash flooding. \n\nWindy conditions possible this weekend from in the southern portion \nof the forecast area including San Bernardino, Clark, and Mohave \nCounties. A stronger mid level jet streak of 35-45 kts moves \noverhead Saturday ahead of the incoming trough. Strong afternoon \nmixing into this jet streak may produce surface wind gusts over 40 \nmph at times. Best day for strongest winds appears to be Saturday, \nbut still watching Sunday and Monday as well as stronger vort max \nassociated with the upper trough axis pushes through which could \nenhance wind gusts.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Breezy \nsoutherly winds (200 to 240 degrees true) with gusts of 15 to 25 \nknots will persist into the early evening. A few isolated \nthunderstorms will continue into the early evening. The threat of a \ndirect impact to the terminal area has decreased; however, scattered \nstorms just east of the Vegas Valley will likely result in \nintermittent impacts to the 26L approaches through sunset. More \ntypical southwest winds are expected overnight. Additional \nconvection is possible Saturday afternoon, although coverage looks \nto be less than what is expected today. Temperatures will exceed \n100F through 04Z this evening and again between 19Z and 02Z \nSaturday. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...In general, winds will \nfollow typical daily directional trends through the TAF period with \noccasional gusts to 25 knots through sunset. The exception to this \nwill be the western Mojave Desert, including KDAG, where winds will \nfavor a more westerly direction through the TAF period, with gusts \npersisting through the evening. Scattered areas of convection will \ncontinue across eastern portions of the region through sunset, with \ngusty erratic winds, lightning, rain, and CIGs below 10kft possible \nin and near any storms. Additional areas of convection are possible \nSaturday, although coverage looks to be less than what is expected \nthis afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           FRI, AUG 28 \n              Record(Yr) \n\nLas Vegas     110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2017)\nNeedles       116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2017)\nKingman       107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     86(2019)* \nBishop        62(1970) \nNeedles       94(2017) \nDaggett       82(2017)* \nKingman       75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley 100(2019)* \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/CLIMATE...Moore\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
